{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-89aaeebc296d74c9","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tstrat_id: RHAISTRAT-9011\n3\tscore: 12\n4\tpass: false\n5\trecommendation: revise\n6\tissues:\n7\t- severity: critical\n8\t  criterion: DAG Coherence\n9\t  description: E010 (docs-authoring) is missing its Rule 11 blocking edge from E001.\n10\t    Rule 11 requires docs-authoring to be blocked by ALL Implementation epics in the\n11\t    strategy, and E010s scope directly documents the convention/JSON schema E001 publishes.\n12\t    Add RHAISTRAT-9011-E001 to E010s dependencies frontmatter and to the summary DAG\n13\t    diagram (currently only E002-E009 -> E010).\n14\t- severity: minor\n15\t  criterion: Epic Boundaries\n16\t  description: 'E001s component field is Documentation, an imprecise attribution for\n17\t    a platform logging-convention design artifact owned by RHOAI Observability; consider\n18\t    a more accurate component mapping or record the conventions destination repo (also\n19\t    flagged by repo_access: 0).'\n20\terror: null\n21\t---\n22\t# Decomposition Review — RHAISTRAT-9011\n23\t\n24\t## Review Summary\n25\t\n26\tScore: 12/14 — fail\n27\tRecommendation: revise\n28\t\n29\tAll seven criteria score 2 except DAG Coherence, which scores 0 due to a single\n30\tcritical issue: the docs-authoring epic (E010) is missing its Rule 11 blocking\n31\tedge from E001. Per the auto-fail rule, any criterion at 0 forces `pass: false`\n32\tregardless of total score.\n33\t\n34\t## Criterion Details\n35\t\n36\t### 1. HLR Coverage (2/2)\n37\t\n38\tTraceability matrix present and complete — all nine HLRs map to at least one\n39\tepic, no orphans. P0 coverage: HLR-1 → E001, HLR-2 → E002, HLR-3 → E003 + E004,\n40\tall P0 epics. Priority inheritance is correct: E001 is P0 because it\n41\ttransitively enables the P0 adoptions. E010's P1 is correctly derived from the\n42\tstrategy's `priority: Major` via the docs-authoring exception (Critical→P0,\n43\tMajor→P1), not from HLR-9's P2 and not inherited from its P0 dependencies —\n44\tthe summary explicitly documents this. No priority collapse: each epic maps to\n45\texactly one HLR; HLR-3 is split across E003/E004 by component, both at P0, so\n46\tnothing deferrable is bundled with P0 work. E009 correctly carries P2 for the\n47\tP2 HLR-8. P2 HLR-9 is covered (not deferred) by E010.\n48\t\n49\t### 2. DAG Coherence (0/2)\n50\t\n51\t**Critical — missing Rule 11 edge E001 → E010.** Rule 11 requires a\n52\tdocs-authoring epic to be blocked by ALL Implementation epics in the strategy.\n53\tE001 is an Implementation epic, and E010's scope directly consumes E001's\n54\tartifact: its first scope bullet is \"a documentation topic describing the\n55\tstructured log format and its required fields (timestamp, level, component,\n56\tnamespace, correlation id)\" — the convention and JSON schema E001 publishes.\n57\tE010's `dependencies` frontmatter lists only E002–E009, and the DAG\n58\tjustification table cites Rule 11 while applying it to only 8 of the 9\n59\tImplementation epics. The ordering happens to be preserved transitively\n60\t(E001 → E002..E009 → E010), but the direct edge is rule-mandated, automated\n61\tconsumers read the `dependencies` list, and a data/artifact dependency\n62\tdiscoverable from epic content (E010 documents E001's schema) without a\n63\tcorresponding edge is a critical issue even though the diagram and frontmatter\n64\tare mutually consistent. Fix: add RHAISTRAT-9011-E001 to E010's dependencies\n65\tand to the summary DAG diagram.\n66\t\n67\tOtherwise the DAG is sound:\n68\t\n69\t- No circular dependencies; every edge references an existing epic.\n70\t- E001 → E002…E009 is a genuine Rule 12/13 build-against dependency (adoption\n71\t  code is written against the convention's schema and per-language library\n72\t  guidance), not a configuration reference, so serial ordering is correct.\n73\t- No edges among E002–E009 (Rule 14: separate repos, no shared artifacts) —\n74\t  matches the strategy's explicit statement that adoptions are independent\n75\t  once the convention exists. Parallel-eligible work is not serialized.\n76\t- No konflux-onboarding, repo-onboarding, or license-validation epics exist,\n77\t  so the mandated-parallelism rules (7, 8, 10) are not implicated; no branch\n78\t  files exist, so branch/gated_by checks do not apply.\n79\t- Diagram arrows run dependency → dependent per convention, and every diagram\n80\t  edge matches the target epic's frontmatter `dependencies` exactly (and vice\n81\t  versa) — verified edge by edge.\n82\t- Critical path length 3 (E001 → any adoption → E010) is appropriate for this\n83\t  fan-out shape; a longer path would be unjustified.\n84\t- Serving Runtimes has no architecture-context file; the decomposition\n85\t  explicitly flags this and justifies treating it as in-platform. Verified:\n86\t  it appears in `.context/rhai-components.txt` and `kserve.md` lists the\n87\t  serving runtimes (vLLM, OpenVINO, Caikit) as an existing platform\n88\t  dependency, so Rule 20's onboarding chain is not triggered. Acceptable,\n89\t  transparently flagged judgment.\n90\t\n91\t### 3. Epic Boundaries (2/2)\n92\t\n93\tOne epic per component/team tuple. Team assignments match the\n94\tarchitecture-context owning teams for all seven components that have files\n95\t(RHOAI Dashboard, RHOAI Serving, RHOAI Pipelines, Model Registry, TrustyAI,\n96\tWorkbenches, Distributed Workloads). E003/E004 correctly split the KServe\n97\tcontroller from Serving Runtimes (same team, different components — Rule 1).\n98\tE005 correctly bundles the DSPO operator and API server (same component, same\n99\tteam, same logical change). E010 is correctly separated from code work per the\n100\twork-product-type rule. No epic exceeds ~2 weeks — each adoption is mechanical\n101\tper the strategy. No scope duplication: the correlation-id work in each epic\n102\tis confined to that component's own request path; no two epics independently\n103\tbuild the same data structure or surface. Minor note: E001's `component:\n104\tDocumentation` is an imprecise attribution for a platform design artifact\n105\towned by RHOAI Observability (acknowledged in the summary's component mapping\n106\tnotes); it does not cause incorrect execution.\n107\t\n108\t### 4. Type Correctness (2/2)\n109\t\n110\tNo Investigation epics — correct: the strategy declares no open questions and\n111\tstates adoptions are mechanical once the convention lands. E001 produces a\n112\tconcrete artifact (the published convention), so Implementation is the right\n113\ttype. E010 is correctly labeled `implementation_type: docs-authoring`. All\n114\t`gated_by`/`gate_failure_impact` fields are null and no epic lists an\n115\tInvestigation in `dependencies`, so there is no gating metadata to mismatch.\n116\t\n117\t### 5. AI Implementability Scoring (2/2)\n118\t\n119\tAll ten epics are Implementation and carry the 9-signal `ai_signals` field\n120\t(no `investigation_signals` misuse); all ten `-ai-signals.md` rationale files\n121\texist and their values match the frontmatter signal-for-signal (cross-checked).\n122\tSignal values are consistent with the strategy content, with several\n123\twell-reasoned distinctions: E004 scores `architecture_claims: 0` (Serving\n124\tRuntimes has no architecture-context file) while components with files score\n125\t+1; E001 scores `human_process_gates: -1` for the cross-team sign-off AC and\n126\t`repo_access: 0` because the destination repo is unspecified. Borderline but\n127\tdefensible calls: `pattern_precedent: +1` on the adoption epics (ad-hoc JSON\n128\tlogging already exists in these codebases) and E001's `open_questions: +1`\n129\t(the strategy explicitly declares none). Per instructions, the computed\n130\t`ai_implementability` totals were not checked.\n131\t\n132\t### 6. Acceptance Criteria Quality (2/2)\n133\t\n134\tEvery epic has three testable acceptance criteria